pts/check-test-immutability.mjs` was failing with three hash mismatches.\\n\\nI re-recorded the manifest using the repository's own tool — `node scripts/record-red-manifest.mjs` — for exactly the three files the quick-fix touched:\\n\\n| File | Why |\\n|------|-----|\\n| `test/work-execution.test.ts` | First-touch leg rewritten: a read now leaves queued rows untouched (`liveCount → 2`) and explicit `work.drain` does recovery |\\n| `test/messages-read.test.ts` | New — carries the DD-6 read-only-delta and zero-provider architecture-risk legs |\\n| `test/cli-process-inspect.test.ts` | New — process-suite list/show parity |\\n\\n## Preserving strict-read-purity\\n\\nI changed **only `test/red-manifest.json`** — I re-recorded hashes to *bless* the quick-fix's edits, never reverting the test bodies. The strict-purity contract is intact in the source (verified): the work-execution leg still asserts `liveCount === 2` after a read with `work.drain` for recovery (\\\"message reads stay pure (Epic 04 DD-6)\\\"), and `messages-read.test.ts` keeps its read-only-delta / zero-provider / no-first-touch-drain assertions.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\n```\\n$ node scripts/check-test-immutability.mjs\\ntest-immutability: OK (43 Red-phase files unchanged)   # exit 0\\n```\\n\\nThe guard (the `check-test-immutability` half of `green-verify`) no longer fails.\\n\\nNote: `test/fixtures/corrupt.ts` and `test/fixtures/index.ts` are also modified in the tree but are fixtures, not manifest-tracked test files, so the guard correctly ignores them — left as-is since they're part of the surrounding story work, not this task.\"}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":909,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":53521,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":909},\"output_tokens\":3,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"},\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_managem